# example_data/

This directory is where `load_data()` reads data from. This package never
downloads anything at runtime: the loader reads what is here and returns it
as a dict of named numpy arrays.

If a `PROVENANCE.json` sits beside this file, R2C bundled a demo-scale
extract of the dataset the paper itself cites (fetched anonymously at build
time, subsampled deterministically). That file records the source, the
checksum of the original fetch, exactly what was kept and cut, and the
honesty note: numbers computed on a subsample demonstrate the method's
mechanism and are not the paper's benchmark results. Replace the bundled
files with the full dataset (or your own) at any time — your files take
priority in the same formats below.

The method's declared entry point (what ultimately consumes this data):

```
{{ interface_signature }}
```

## Supported formats

Drop a file into this directory. `load_data` checks formats in this priority
order: `.npz` → `.json` → delimited tables (`.csv` and `.tsv`).

### Option 1 — `.npz` (fastest)

A numpy archive (any name); every array in it is returned under its key:

```python
np.savez("example_data/my_dataset.npz", x=..., y=...)
```

### Option 2 — `.json`

A single `.json` file (any name) mapping names to nested numeric lists:

```json
{
  "x": [[0.1, 0.5], [0.3, 0.2]],
  "y": [3, 7]
}
```

### Option 3 — `.csv` or `.tsv`

A single 2-D numeric table, returned under the key `"data"`. TSV uses the
same contract with a tab delimiter.

If several files of one format exist, the alphabetically first is used.
